{
  "dom_id": "step-q126269",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226762/assets/359e831be2c58ffa4d121a764bf5fb1845c90da4c2b131fecaf7cd8a7488e759.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ as shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have two solutions: \n\n$\\,$ $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}$    \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226762/assets/5e4d8d6f5a3edae1df62d8f1e848f6a1cb04b25db170b3a4965181c9bda4297b.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$    \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
      "key": "selection",
      "type": "radio",
      "choices": [
